{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And he struck your rear guard.</b> That is, he cut off your tail — and these are the stragglers behind you, the weak ones who had no strength to walk. And so is the way of camps pursued by enemies: when the enemies lack the strength to breach the camp, they strike at the rear and attack those who are last in the camp, who are the weakest of all. This is its plain sense." "<b>And its Midrash:</b> ויזנב בך — the striking of the tail: he would cut off the foreskin of circumcision and fling it upward and say, 'Take what you have chosen.' And he would do this to those lacking strength — the tails and the weak among them — because of their sins, for the Cloud was expelling them. <b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Remember what Amalek did to you' — this is what Scripture says (Job 13:12): 'Your remembrances are like proverbs of ashes compared to the clay of your hills.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Those two remembrances that I wrote for you in the Torah — be careful with them. One is here, and one is (Exodus 17:14): 'כי מחה אמחה את זכר עמלק.' If you do so, you will be the children of Abraham, who likened himself to ashes, as it says (Genesis 18:27): 'ואנכי עפר ואפר.' And if not — 'compared to the clay of your hills' — prepare yourselves for the servitude of Egypt, as it is written (Exodus 1:14): 'בחמר ובלבנים,' (Psalms 79:12): 'והשב לשכנינו שבעתים אל חיקם חרפתם אשר חרפוך ה׳.' What would the house of Amalek do? They would slice off their circumcisions and fling them upward and say, 'Here, take what you have chosen,' as is written above in the matter: 'והחזיקה במבושיו.' Samuel, of blessed memory, came and paid them back, as it says (I Samuel 15:33): 'ושסף שמואל את אגג לפני ה׳' — he would cut his flesh like olives and feed it to the birds of prey. What is 'Amalek'? A people that came to lick the blood of Israel like a dog. Rabbi Levi said in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Chalfta: To what was Amalek likened? To a fly that is drawn after a wound — so was Amalek drawn after Israel. 'On the way, when you came out of Egypt' — a parable: a king who had a vineyard, and he fenced it round and placed a biting dog in it. The king said: 'Whoever comes and breaches the fence, the dog will bite him.' After some days the king's son came and breached the fence, and the dog came and bit him. Whenever the king wished to remind his son of his sin of breaching the fence, he would say to him: 'Remember how the dog bit you.' So, whenever the Holy One, blessed be He, seeks to remind Israel of their sin in the things they said (Exodus 17:7): 'היש ה׳ בקרבנו אם אין,' He says to them: 'Remember what Amalek did to you.'" "<b>And did not fear Elohim.</b> This refers back to Amalek: for while Israel was weary at Rephidim and parched for water, Amalek came to destroy them." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'And did not fear Elohim' — it is a tradition of Aggadah that Esau is destined to fall into the hands of the children of Rachel, as it says (Jeremiah 49:20): 'אם לא יסחבום צעירי הצאן' — these are the youngest of the tribes. Concerning this one it is written 'na'ar' (נער) and concerning that one it is written 'katan' (קטן): concerning this one 'na'ar' is written, as it says (Genesis 37): 'והוא נער,' and concerning that one 'katan' is written, as it says (Obadiah): 'הנה קטן נתתיך וגו׳.' This one grew up between two righteous men and did not learn from their deeds, and that one grew up between two wicked men and did not learn from their deeds — let this one fall into the hand of that one. Concerning this one it is written (Genesis 42:18): 'את האלהים אני ירא,' and concerning that one it is written: 'ולא ירא אלהים' — let this one fall into the hand of that one."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:15.228941187Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}